/* ============================================================
   Login-portal scenes — the two isometric illustrations in the
   nav's "Log in" dropdown.

   ▸ GENERATED, NOT DRAWN ◂ The geometry comes from the user's
   gen_portal_scenes.py (a true dimetric isometric projection);
   this file is a mechanical JSX transcription of its two SVG
   outputs. Do NOT nudge a polygon here — the next regeneration
   overwrites it. Change the generator, re-export, re-convert.

   ▸ COLOUR ◂ Every fill is a --il-* custom property, defined once
   on .iz-login-card in iznav.css with a dark override, so one copy
   of the geometry serves both themes. The accent pair is set per
   scene on the root: ZTA is orange throughout; i365 takes the
   card's own --lg-accent (purple, which the card already swaps for
   dark) with --il-acc2 held at orange for the payload cube and the
   one cross-product beam.

   ▸ IDS ARE SUFFIXED ◂ Both scenes define gGlow / gGlowV / gPlane /
   gBeamV. They render in the same document, so a bare id would let
   the second scene's url(#gGlow) resolve to the first scene's
   gradient — and since those gradients read var(--il-acc), i365
   would silently glow orange. Every id and reference carries a
   -zta / -i365 suffix. Keep it that way.

   The nav still renders each scene twice — once in the desktop
   dropdown, once in the mobile sheet — so a suffixed id does appear
   twice per document and the second copy's url(#…) resolves to the
   first copy's gradient. That is harmless by construction, not by
   luck: every gGlow-zta node sits inside an orange card and every
   gGlow-i365 inside a purple one, so whichever instance wins the
   lookup carries the same accent. Only a per-instance suffix would
   remove the duplicate, and that would mean LOGIN_PORTALS holding
   component references instead of elements.

   .beam and .iris are animation hooks; iznav.css drives them on
   card hover only, behind prefers-reduced-motion.
   ============================================================ */

